  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 208 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Over the past few days, the signals for showers \nassociated with a vigorous shortwave trough have been uncertain for \nFriday. There had been a trend for no precipitation with too much \ndry air in place ahead of this wave, but the trend in recent model \nruns has reversed such that it is becoming likely that yet another \ninterval of isolated to scattered showers with perhaps a \nthunderstorm or two will traverse our region. This time, it seems \nlike only our southern areas, primarily the southern half of St. \nLawrence County and perhaps as far north as portions of the northern \nAdirondacks and south central Vermont, are in the crosshairs. \nRepresentative forecast soundings show only about 400 - 600 J/kg \nCAPE ahead of the showers, yielding a lower chance of thunderstorms \nthan in previous days. The associated low hourly probabilities of \nthunder in the HREF-calibrated guidance (barely reaching 10% in a \nsmall swath of the aforementioned area between 4 and 5 PM) look \nreasonable. As in recent days, inverted-V soundings suggest that the \nshowers and thunderstorms will produce gusty winds with perhaps a \nstronger wind gust with an isolated storm. The lower CAPE further \nreduces the chances of any severe weather today where these showers \nand storms track. \n\nFinally surface high pressure builds in enough to bring chances\nof rain down to zero areawide this evening through Sunday \nmorning. As such, through this period seasonally typical valley \nfog each morning will provide the only potential weather hazard.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Confidence continues to increase on a period of \nwidespread showers with a few downpours in the Sunday \nevening/overnight timeframe as an area of low pressure passes near \nor south of our region. The latest blend of guidance shows area \naverage rainfall near 0.5\" to locally 0.75\" for most locations, with \nmost of it occurring during the first part of the night per model \nconsensus. Ensemble support for heavy rainfall continues to focus \nwell to our south, but we will be on the northern fringe of well \nabove normal precipitable water coincident with a surge of \nintegrated water vapor transport out of the west. The most anomalous \nmoisture appears to be at the mid-levels/700 millibars where strong \nwarm air advection will occur, which is consistent with forecast \nsoundings showing elevated instability above that layer. So some \nrumbles of thunder will be possible despite minimal surface-based \ninstability during the event, with a tongue of this instability \ngenerally pushing northeastward with time Sunday evening per \nconsensus of the 00Z guidance.\n\nThe situation for Monday is hard to parse with regards to how the \nweather will evolve. It continues to look like there will be a \ntrough digging slightly across Quebec that will drag a weak cold \nfront through our region in the wake of the low pressure system \ndiscussed previously. Near surface moisture should increase somewhat \nwhile deep moisture decreases relative to Sunday night, and diurnal \nheating should lead to some destabilization as a boundary or \nboundaries pass through. Forecast soundings suggest there may be too \nmuch dry air aloft for parcels of air to rise sufficiently to \nproduce cold cloud tops, so at this point a slight chance of \nthunderstorms looks reasonable. Altogether, there are mixed \nsignals on thunderstorm chances but scattered showers seem \nreasonable with precipitation most favored in higher terrain. \nThe blend of models shows only around 20% chance of thunder \nMonday afternoon in Vermont and closer to 10% in northern New \nYork.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Pretty much remain in a broad cyclonic flow that is \ndominating the Great Lakes-NY-New England. Heat ridge remains mainly \nacross southern-southwest tier of CONUS. Our CWA is far enough from \nthe periphery of the ridge that we stay largely influenced by \nthe northern stream flow with seasonable temperatures and a \nchance of showers now and then. The best chance for showers \nafter Sun-Mon departing system will be late Wed-Thu.",
